WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.840
All right, we'll start.

2
00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:39.560
Does anybody not hear?

3
00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:42.560
You're all here?

4
00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:54.200
Okay, let's do drug toxicities on page 26.

5
00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:56.200
Bad news, you got to know your drug toxicities.

6
00:00:56.200 --> 00:00:59.600
Good news, you don't have to know them all.

7
00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:02.560
You just need to know what everybody else knows.

8
00:01:02.560 --> 00:01:06.840
And if anybody knows drug toxicities, they know these five.

9
00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:11.480
Anything beyond these five is really nuts to know.

10
00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:14.700
It's not really need to know territory.

11
00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:16.480
You do need to know these five though.

12
00:01:16.480 --> 00:01:22.320
I would really suggest that this is a little bit of memory work, but it's a good payoff

13
00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:24.400
for it.

14
00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:29.000
Because you understand this is a test for nurse safety.

15
00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:34.680
And one of the biggest ways you can be unsafe is to not know drug toxic effects.

16
00:01:34.680 --> 00:01:38.720
You see how that could result in real patient harm.

17
00:01:38.720 --> 00:01:46.480
So that's an area they like to test is toxic reactions to drugs, drug toxicities.

18
00:01:46.480 --> 00:01:49.000
So let's talk about these five.

19
00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:50.840
Lithium.

20
00:01:50.840 --> 00:01:57.680
Lithium of course is the famous anti-mania drug.

21
00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:00.880
It's used for bipolar, but it's not used for the depression.

22
00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:05.240
It's used for the mania.

23
00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:08.720
It's therapeutic level in the middle column there.

24
00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:20.040
You should write 0.6 to 1.2.

25
00:02:20.040 --> 00:02:27.120
Now the toxic level is greater than or equal to 2.

26
00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:32.080
Now immediately you should see that there is a gray area there where you're higher than

27
00:02:32.080 --> 00:02:34.760
1.2, but under 2.0.

28
00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:37.320
You see that gray area?

29
00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:43.400
That is a gray area where no books agree on exactly what's going on with lithium at those

30
00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:44.920
levels.

31
00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:51.040
From 0.6 to 1.2 everyone agrees that it is therapeutic.

32
00:02:51.040 --> 00:02:54.680
Everyone agrees that two and over is toxic.

33
00:02:54.680 --> 00:03:01.840
Now in between you just pick your book and they're going to say different things.

34
00:03:01.840 --> 00:03:05.560
One of the things that you guys don't know about me, well maybe you do know about me

35
00:03:05.560 --> 00:03:12.280
that probably is good for you to know about me and help you understand why I say what

36
00:03:12.280 --> 00:03:22.440
I do and how I say what I do is that for years I was an NCLEX RN item test writer.

37
00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:26.440
I wrote questions on the NCLEX.

38
00:03:26.440 --> 00:03:32.920
So the questions that were on there I've written some of those questions.

39
00:03:32.920 --> 00:03:41.040
So I've been through the whole training protocol on how they want you to write questions for

40
00:03:41.040 --> 00:03:42.040
them.

41
00:03:42.040 --> 00:03:45.960
So when I tell you that questions are written this way that's because I've been through

42
00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:49.680
the in-service education on it and I've been trained as their item writer.

43
00:03:49.680 --> 00:03:56.320
In 2003 I think it was, maybe it was 2002, somewhere right around in there the NCSBN

44
00:03:56.320 --> 00:04:10.000
made a rule that you cannot be an item writer and be a NCLEX reviewer at the same time.

45
00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:11.000
Which makes sense.

46
00:04:11.000 --> 00:04:15.600
You can't write the questions and then go teach people how to pass the test.

47
00:04:15.600 --> 00:04:18.680
It's not ethically correct.

48
00:04:18.680 --> 00:04:26.120
So at that time I had to pick between writing questions and reviewing and I picked reviewing

49
00:04:26.120 --> 00:04:34.240
and so I stopped writing questions and so I haven't written a question since 2003 roughly.

50
00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:39.560
But if they only change 5% every year there's still loads of questions on there that I've

51
00:04:39.560 --> 00:04:40.880
written.

52
00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:49.120
Now I say that to tell you that when I give you ranges, the ranges I give you may not

53
00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:56.040
be the ones you learned but they are the ones that you use when you write questions.

54
00:04:56.040 --> 00:04:58.040
Did you see what I'm saying?

55
00:04:58.040 --> 00:05:03.320
And most of the time my ranges are wider than what you, most of the times than what you

56
00:05:03.320 --> 00:05:08.200
learned because the instructions are to every item writer on the boards is when you write

57
00:05:08.200 --> 00:05:16.280
a test question where you use a lab value or a range, a value in a range, a number,

58
00:05:16.280 --> 00:05:21.440
go to at least five different books published by five different publishers and see what

59
00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:22.840
they say.

60
00:05:22.840 --> 00:05:28.640
The ranges then make your range wide enough to fit all of those.

61
00:05:28.640 --> 00:05:30.720
Did you see what I'm saying?

62
00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:37.720
So the books one says one to two, book two says one to 2.3, book three says 0.8 to 1.8

63
00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:39.760
to 1.9.

64
00:05:39.760 --> 00:05:44.880
Well you've got to go from 0.8 to 2.3 as your range.

65
00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:49.120
That way it's a fair question no matter what book the student's used.

66
00:05:49.120 --> 00:05:55.120
So you're going to see my ranges are different but my ranges are made to fit the NCLEX.

67
00:05:55.120 --> 00:05:57.360
Do you understand that?

68
00:05:57.360 --> 00:06:00.040
And not necessarily a particular book.

69
00:06:00.040 --> 00:06:05.640
So you may use my ranges and my numbers and you may go take the, like the Saunders review

70
00:06:05.640 --> 00:06:10.200
book with questions or the exam cram with questions or the NCLEX gold with questions

71
00:06:10.200 --> 00:06:15.200
and using my ranges you may miss the question.

72
00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:18.680
Why?

73
00:06:18.680 --> 00:06:20.160
Because they went by their ranges.

74
00:06:20.160 --> 00:06:20.660
What ranges?

75
00:06:20.660 --> 00:06:24.120
The ranges in the MedSearch book that that company publishes.

76
00:06:24.120 --> 00:06:26.200
Only one book, not five.

77
00:06:26.200 --> 00:06:28.280
Now Kaplan does what I do.

78
00:06:28.280 --> 00:06:34.560
Anybody that's a national reviewer does what I do which is a wider range than normal.

79
00:06:34.560 --> 00:06:36.400
So here's the deal.

80
00:06:36.400 --> 00:06:46.400
Boards would never give you a lithium of 1.3, 1.4, 1.5, 1.6, 1.7, 1.8, 1.9.

81
00:06:46.400 --> 00:06:47.400
Why?

82
00:06:47.400 --> 00:06:54.080
It's in that gray area where books don't agree and item writers have to get it to where all

83
00:06:54.080 --> 00:06:55.400
the books agree.

84
00:06:55.400 --> 00:07:00.560
So I teach you two and greater because every book agrees that that is toxic.

85
00:07:00.560 --> 00:07:01.980
Alrighty?

86
00:07:01.980 --> 00:07:05.060
So don't worry about that when you see these ranges.

87
00:07:05.060 --> 00:07:06.060
Okay, lanoxone.

88
00:07:06.060 --> 00:07:08.420
Lanoxone, what's the other name for it?

89
00:07:08.420 --> 00:07:10.820
I told you yesterday you have to know this.

90
00:07:10.820 --> 00:07:11.980
DIG.

91
00:07:11.980 --> 00:07:14.900
This is used to treat basically two things.

92
00:07:14.900 --> 00:07:21.700
DIG is used to treat afib and congestive heart fib.

93
00:07:21.700 --> 00:07:25.420
Atrial fib and congestive heart failure.

94
00:07:25.420 --> 00:07:31.820
How should you have already known that it treats atrial fib?

95
00:07:31.820 --> 00:07:34.980
DIG.

96
00:07:34.980 --> 00:07:39.100
In what context did we talk about DIG yesterday?

97
00:07:39.100 --> 00:07:49.620
Adena, beta, calcium, DIG.

98
00:07:49.620 --> 00:07:53.980
So DIG is the D of the ABCDs of atrial treatment.

99
00:07:53.980 --> 00:07:56.340
So atrial fib, yes.

100
00:07:56.340 --> 00:08:00.860
And then congestive heart failure is the other big one that it treats.

101
00:08:00.860 --> 00:08:04.420
Well, what is the therapeutic level of DIG?

102
00:08:04.420 --> 00:08:05.900
One to two.

103
00:08:05.900 --> 00:08:08.500
And you notice I'm not giving you units, correct?

104
00:08:08.500 --> 00:08:11.220
Because what did I tell you about units?

105
00:08:11.220 --> 00:08:12.220
Don't worry about it.

106
00:08:12.220 --> 00:08:13.820
They're not going to ask you.

107
00:08:13.820 --> 00:08:17.500
They're going to say, how many milligrams per deciliter of?

108
00:08:17.500 --> 00:08:21.380
And you just say the number.

109
00:08:21.380 --> 00:08:29.220
This toxic level is greater than or equal to two.

110
00:08:29.220 --> 00:08:33.060
So what do you notice about two?

111
00:08:33.060 --> 00:08:36.460
It could be therapeutic and it could be toxic.

112
00:08:36.460 --> 00:08:40.460
So what do you call it when they give you a value of two?

113
00:08:40.460 --> 00:08:42.820
Toxic why?

114
00:08:42.820 --> 00:08:48.300
It's safer to call something toxic when it may not be than to say it's therapeutic when

115
00:08:48.300 --> 00:08:50.220
it might not be.

116
00:08:50.220 --> 00:08:55.140
So always err on that safe side.

117
00:08:55.140 --> 00:08:58.060
What do you notice about lithium and linoxins toxicities?

118
00:08:58.060 --> 00:09:02.660
They're the same at two and greater.

119
00:09:02.660 --> 00:09:03.660
Aminophilin.

120
00:09:03.660 --> 00:09:07.860
Aminophilin is a airway anti-spasmodic.

121
00:09:07.860 --> 00:09:11.380
It relieves spasms in your airway.

122
00:09:11.380 --> 00:09:14.580
It is technically not a bronchodilator.

123
00:09:14.580 --> 00:09:15.580
What do I mean by that?

124
00:09:15.580 --> 00:09:20.660
It's technically not a bronchodilator.

125
00:09:20.660 --> 00:09:24.780
What does it not do?

126
00:09:24.780 --> 00:09:27.260
It doesn't stimulate your beta-2 agonist cell.

127
00:09:27.260 --> 00:09:30.860
It's just bronchodilate.

128
00:09:30.860 --> 00:09:32.740
It just relaxes a spasm.

129
00:09:32.740 --> 00:09:35.060
Now when you're in a spasm, your airway is what?

130
00:09:35.060 --> 00:09:36.100
Narrow or wide?

131
00:09:36.100 --> 00:09:40.260
And when I relax a spasm, what happens is the airway gets what?

132
00:09:40.260 --> 00:09:41.260
Wider.

133
00:09:41.260 --> 00:09:46.820
It actually looks like it's a bronchodilator, but it's not really a bronchodilator.

134
00:09:46.820 --> 00:09:51.100
Epinephrine is a bronchodilator.

135
00:09:51.100 --> 00:09:53.100
Hypotroprin is a bronchodilator.

136
00:09:53.100 --> 00:09:55.060
Alupent, albuterol is it?

137
00:09:55.060 --> 00:09:56.060
Arbongodilator.

138
00:09:56.060 --> 00:09:57.900
Turbutylin is a bronchodilator.

139
00:09:57.900 --> 00:10:00.860
This is a muscle spasm relaxer.

140
00:10:00.860 --> 00:10:04.740
Has anybody ever, and I'm sort of just going off the side here on these things just to

141
00:10:04.740 --> 00:10:06.620
see if you can apply some stuff.

142
00:10:06.620 --> 00:10:10.860
Have you ever seen anybody come in or heard of anyone coming in in an acute asthmatic

143
00:10:10.860 --> 00:10:15.580
attack and the bronchodilators aren't working?

144
00:10:15.580 --> 00:10:16.580
That happens on occasion.

145
00:10:16.580 --> 00:10:17.580
Probably what's happening.

146
00:10:17.580 --> 00:10:20.580
Why are the bronchodilators working?

147
00:10:20.580 --> 00:10:22.580
What's that?

148
00:10:22.580 --> 00:10:27.180
I really can't hear you, Mr. Trump.

149
00:10:27.180 --> 00:10:32.180
Okay, the inflammation, but there's a bigger reason.

150
00:10:32.180 --> 00:10:34.380
They're in a spasm.

151
00:10:34.380 --> 00:10:37.460
They're in acute, locked down spasm.

152
00:10:37.460 --> 00:10:40.780
And the bronchodilator isn't going to work because the spasm is in the way.

153
00:10:40.780 --> 00:10:45.020
So what do you have to give them first?

154
00:10:45.020 --> 00:10:49.260
Some aminophilin to do what to the spasm?

155
00:10:49.260 --> 00:10:50.260
Relieve the spasm.

156
00:10:50.260 --> 00:10:53.780
Then you can come in with a bronchodilator and it will work.

157
00:10:53.780 --> 00:11:00.740
So just remember when a bronchodilator doesn't work in an acute airway problem, give them

158
00:11:00.740 --> 00:11:05.580
a theophilin, give them an aminophilin, and that will melt the spasm and then the bronchodilator

159
00:11:05.580 --> 00:11:06.580
will work.

160
00:11:06.580 --> 00:11:07.580
Yes?

161
00:11:07.580 --> 00:11:16.820
A lot of times when little kids come in with acute, reactive airway disease, they'll slap

162
00:11:16.820 --> 00:11:22.060
them on an aminophilin drip and then start giving them breathing treatments with betas.

163
00:11:22.060 --> 00:11:24.260
And they may have 20 of them.

164
00:11:24.260 --> 00:11:29.180
And the first 19 may not work until that aminophilin kicks in and then the 19th one works.

165
00:11:29.180 --> 00:11:31.180
You see?

166
00:11:31.180 --> 00:11:32.180
So I've seen little kids.

167
00:11:32.180 --> 00:11:36.820
What's the most breathing treatments you've seen in airway?

168
00:11:36.820 --> 00:11:41.260
Have you ever in work ER in children's stuff ever?

169
00:11:41.260 --> 00:11:42.260
What's the most?

170
00:11:42.260 --> 00:11:43.260
Nobody's...

171
00:11:43.260 --> 00:11:44.260
Okay.

172
00:11:44.260 --> 00:11:45.260
Well then it's a no question.

173
00:11:45.260 --> 00:11:49.220
But I have seen up to 20 breathing treatments being given in a row for these little kids.

174
00:11:49.220 --> 00:11:51.060
It's really just phenomenal.

175
00:11:51.060 --> 00:11:52.060
Okay.

176
00:11:52.060 --> 00:11:53.940
That's not typical.

177
00:11:53.940 --> 00:11:54.940
That's the extreme.

178
00:11:54.940 --> 00:11:55.940
All right.

179
00:11:55.940 --> 00:11:56.940
What's the therapeutic level?

180
00:11:56.940 --> 00:11:59.940
10 to 20.

181
00:11:59.940 --> 00:12:01.340
There again, don't worry about units.

182
00:12:01.340 --> 00:12:02.980
10 to 20.

183
00:12:02.980 --> 00:12:05.580
Any aminophilin under 10 is what?

184
00:12:06.340 --> 00:12:10.740
If you get an aminophilin level of 8, what's that mean?

185
00:12:10.740 --> 00:12:12.780
So they need to take more.

186
00:12:12.780 --> 00:12:15.340
Or are they taking it?

187
00:12:15.340 --> 00:12:16.340
Okay.

188
00:12:16.340 --> 00:12:18.340
If it's 21, what's it mean?

189
00:12:18.340 --> 00:12:19.340
It's toxic.

190
00:12:19.340 --> 00:12:21.620
But anything in between 10 and 20 is therapeutic.

191
00:12:21.620 --> 00:12:24.540
What if it's 20?

192
00:12:24.540 --> 00:12:28.260
Because 20 toxicity is greater than or equal to 20 on this one.

193
00:12:28.260 --> 00:12:33.340
So 20 could be either way, so you call it toxic if you have to.

194
00:12:33.340 --> 00:12:36.620
So aminophilin toxic is greater than or equal to 20.

195
00:12:36.620 --> 00:12:38.900
Dilanthin, what is this used for?

196
00:12:38.900 --> 00:12:41.660
Does anybody know what dilanthin or phenytoin?

197
00:12:41.660 --> 00:12:42.660
Seizure.

198
00:12:42.660 --> 00:12:44.700
Seizure, yeah.

199
00:12:44.700 --> 00:12:47.020
Its therapeutic level is 10 to 20.

200
00:12:47.020 --> 00:12:51.860
And its toxicity is greater than or equal to 20.

201
00:12:51.860 --> 00:12:52.860
So what do you call 20?

202
00:12:52.860 --> 00:12:53.860
Not faster.

203
00:12:53.860 --> 00:12:59.100
Although it may be therapeutic.

204
00:12:59.100 --> 00:13:00.100
Billie Rubin.

205
00:13:00.100 --> 00:13:01.620
Billie Rubin is not a drug.

206
00:13:01.620 --> 00:13:07.260
It's a waste product from the breakdown of red blood cells.

207
00:13:07.260 --> 00:13:12.220
When boards test Billie Rubin, they will only test it in newborns.

208
00:13:12.220 --> 00:13:14.060
They will never test it in adults.

209
00:13:14.060 --> 00:13:18.020
Does anybody know what the normal adult Billie Rubin is?

210
00:13:18.020 --> 00:13:20.180
It's like 1 to 2.

211
00:13:20.180 --> 00:13:23.260
You know, 1.4 to 2.3, something like that.

212
00:13:23.260 --> 00:13:24.260
It's really low.

213
00:13:24.260 --> 00:13:27.580
But in newborns, newborns have high Billie Rubens all the time.

214
00:13:27.580 --> 00:13:28.580
Why?

215
00:13:28.580 --> 00:13:29.580
What are they doing?

216
00:13:29.580 --> 00:13:30.580
Breaking down.

217
00:13:31.020 --> 00:13:32.420
Which red blood cells?

218
00:13:32.420 --> 00:13:33.420
The moths.

219
00:13:33.420 --> 00:13:38.500
So babies will run Billie Rubens at 5, 6, 7, 8, 9.

220
00:13:38.500 --> 00:13:39.500
And that's fine.

221
00:13:39.500 --> 00:13:44.940
Now, if I had an 8 Billie Rubin right now, they'd have my rear end in a bed over there

222
00:13:44.940 --> 00:13:47.100
finding out what's wrong with me.

223
00:13:47.100 --> 00:13:50.200
But a newborn with an 8 is no big deal.

224
00:13:50.200 --> 00:13:53.460
So what I want you to do with this Billie Rubin is you see where it says therapeutic

225
00:13:53.460 --> 00:13:55.860
level, the middle column.

226
00:13:55.860 --> 00:14:00.420
In the box for Billie Rubin, I want you to write the word elevated level.

227
00:14:01.420 --> 00:14:05.420
So what is the elevated range for Billie Rubin in a newborn?

228
00:14:05.420 --> 00:14:07.420
10 to 20.

229
00:14:09.420 --> 00:14:16.420
So what does it mean when a Billie Rubin in a newborn is 9.9 and less?

230
00:14:18.420 --> 00:14:23.420
It's normal, but it's high for an adult, but it's normal.

231
00:14:23.420 --> 00:14:29.420
So we don't even get concerned about newborn Billie Rubens until it hits what number?

232
00:14:31.420 --> 00:14:32.420
10.

233
00:14:32.420 --> 00:14:36.420
Which is 10 times 5 to 10 times more than what an adult should have.

234
00:14:38.420 --> 00:14:41.420
So what is the Billie Rubin toxicity?

235
00:14:41.420 --> 00:14:43.420
Greater than or equal to 20.

236
00:14:47.420 --> 00:14:55.420
So when do you suppose a child, a child with what Billie Rubin and above do you think needs

237
00:14:55.420 --> 00:14:57.420
to come into the hospital?

238
00:15:01.420 --> 00:15:04.420
Certainly before, what about before 10?

239
00:15:05.420 --> 00:15:06.420
No.

240
00:15:06.420 --> 00:15:10.420
And even at 10, 11 and 12, what are they?

241
00:15:10.420 --> 00:15:11.420
Is it elevated?

242
00:15:12.420 --> 00:15:18.420
But you can manage it at home with sunlight, drinking a lot, flushing that Billie Rubin out.

243
00:15:18.420 --> 00:15:26.420
It's usually right about 14 to 15 that docs start thinking about hospitalizing these kids.

244
00:15:27.420 --> 00:15:29.420
About 14 or 15.

245
00:15:29.420 --> 00:15:35.420
Because once you hit 15, where are you in the scheme of things overall?

246
00:15:37.420 --> 00:15:38.420
You're halfway to what?

247
00:15:38.420 --> 00:15:40.420
You're halfway to toxic.

248
00:15:40.420 --> 00:15:42.420
And once you hit toxic, they can die.

249
00:15:42.420 --> 00:15:46.420
So you don't want it to be 18 or 17 or 19.

